From 447e12e227d63de49fd461c071e9cca774345ad1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 18:34:59 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] dos: unconditionally yield once per frame via SDL_Delay(0)

The frame limiter (patch 0006/afc7239) only called SDL_Delay when
extraTime > 0 -- i.e. only when the frame finished under the 15fps
budget with time to spare. On a 486DX2-66 + S3 ViRGE real-hardware
rig, this game's own measured fps during actual gameplay (12.41-
13.25, across every video-mode config tested) never clears that
budget, so extraTime is negative on every single frame and the
SDL_Delay call was never reached at all during real gameplay on
that class of machine -- the main loop never yielded once.

This backend's cooperative thread model (setjmp/longjmp-based, see
the existing DOS-PORT comment just above this change) depends on
SDL_Delay's DOS_Yield() hook to give other cooperative 'threads' --
notably the audio mixer -- any CPU time at all. Thread::staticSleep
does not do this (raw usleep, bypasses SDL entirely); only SDL_Delay
does. A zero-length SDL_Delay(0) still triggers a real DOS_Yield()
context-switch, independent of whether the frame pacer itself needs
to sleep for any nonzero duration.

Sibling DOS port doskutsu's Renderer::flip() calls SDL_Delay(0)
unconditionally on every flip, with no guard, for exactly this
reason, and has been validated clean across dozens of sustained
real-hardware sessions on this same card model. This patch brings
dossage's own per-frame yield in line with that precedent: the
existing real-sleep behavior when extraTime > 0 is unchanged, but
the else branch now calls SDL_Delay(0) instead of skipping the call
entirely, so the cooperative scheduler gets a chance to run the
audio thread on every frame regardless of how loaded the frame was.

This is being investigated as a candidate contributor to a video-
corruption symptom observed on real hardware during sustained
gameplay (see PLAN.md's Phase 0/2 write-up) -- a starved cooperative
scheduler is a plausible mechanism for state that only goes wrong
under sustained real-time load, though it is not yet confirmed as
the corruption's root cause. This patch is a minimal, narrowly
scoped change to restore the yield; it does not otherwise restructure
the frame-pacing loop.
---
 gameSource/game.cpp | 37 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gameSource/game.cpp b/gameSource/game.cpp
index a32c478..06dca89 100644
--- a/gameSource/game.cpp
+++ b/gameSource/game.cpp
@@ -1491,17 +1491,38 @@ char playGame() {
         
         double extraTime = 1.0 / lockedFrameRate - frameTime;
         
+        // DOS-PORT: SDL_Delay, not Thread::staticSleep -- on DOS,
+        // Thread::staticSleep is a raw usleep() that bypasses SDL
+        // entirely (see ThreadDOS.cpp). This backend's cooperative
+        // audio-thread scheduler only gets CPU time via SDL_Delay
+        // (its DOS_Yield hook); without this, the audio device opens
+        // fine but PlayDevice/WaitDevice never run at all, and
+        // nothing pushed into the ring is ever mixed or played,
+        // regardless of what the audio callback computes.
+        //
+        // DOS-PORT: this call must happen every frame, unconditionally,
+        // not just when extraTime > 0. On slower real hardware (e.g. a
+        // 486DX2-66), sustained frameTime already exceeds the 15fps
+        // budget on every single frame, so extraTime is always negative
+        // and a guarded-only call (the prior form of this code) meant
+        // the loop never yielded at all during real gameplay on that
+        // class of machine -- SDL_Delay(0) still triggers this backend's
+        // DOS_Yield() cooperative context-switch even at a zero-length
+        // sleep, which is what the audio-thread scheduler above actually
+        // depends on, independent of how long (if at all) the frame
+        // pacer needs to sleep. This matches sibling DOS port doskutsu's
+        // Renderer::flip(), which calls SDL_Delay(0) unconditionally on
+        // every flip for the same reason. Confirmed by real-hardware
+        // testing on a 486DX2-66 + S3 ViRGE rig: with the guarded-only
+        // call, that machine's own measured fps (12.41-13.25) never
+        // clears the 15fps budget, so extraTime was negative on every
+        // frame and SDL_Delay was never reached during gameplay at all.
         if( extraTime > 0 ) {
-            // DOS-PORT: SDL_Delay, not Thread::staticSleep -- on DOS,
-            // Thread::staticSleep is a raw usleep() that bypasses SDL
-            // entirely (see ThreadDOS.cpp). This backend's cooperative
-            // audio-thread scheduler only gets CPU time via SDL_Delay
-            // (its DOS_Yield hook); without this, the audio device opens
-            // fine but PlayDevice/WaitDevice never run at all, and
-            // nothing pushed into the ring is ever mixed or played,
-            // regardless of what the audio callback computes.
             SDL_Delay( (Uint32)( extraTime * 1000 ) );
             }
+        else {
+            SDL_Delay( 0 );
+            }
 
         // start timing next frame
         lastFrameTimeStamp = Time::getCurrentTime();
-- 
